# `i < to`, to a number in a float register: in front of the loop the number is made an integer bound: rounded up or down and truncated. NaN and what lies beyond 2^62 were then chosen for by three choices with their constants, at every entry: 14 instructions and 76 bytes,
# though the walk asks next whether the bound is an int32. What the truncation gives is taken where it is an int32 (NaN and what lies outside truncate to none); the rest is left to the run time on a way of its own.
